# InterWeave {version}

This release of InterWeave is for Android phones and for Linux
computers. Each has its own file. Read the section for the device that
you use.

InterWeave is not in any app store. You install it and update it
yourself, from its release pages.

## What is new

{release_notes}

## Android

Download [`interweave-{version}-arm64-v8a.apk`]({apk_url}).

It runs on Android 11 or later, on a phone with a 64-bit ARM processor
(`arm64-v8a`). On any other phone, Android does not install it.

Use these two values to check that the file is the one published here:

- SHA-256 of the file: `{apk_sha256}`
- SHA-256 of the signing certificate, the same for every release:
  `{cert_sha256}`

The app also contains Rust libraries. Their licence notices are in a
separate file, attached to this release (see "Third-party notices
for the Android app" further down this page). Use this value to
check that the notices file is the one published here:

- SHA-256 of the notices file: `{crate_notices_sha256}`

### Installing and updating on Android

To install it, open the downloaded file on your phone. If you
downloaded it on a computer, copy it to the phone first. Android may
say that your browser or file manager cannot install apps. Then tap
Settings, turn on "Allow from this source", go back, and tap Install.
These words can be different on some phones.

To update, download a newer release from its page and install it in
the same way. Android updates the app you have, and your messages and
your identity stay on the phone. The app does not check for updates,
so it cannot tell you that a new release exists. Android does not
install an older release over a newer one.

Do not uninstall the app to update it. If you uninstall it, Android
deletes all of its data from the phone. This includes your messages
and your identity.

On a computer, you can check the downloaded file before you install
it:

- The file's SHA-256 (for example, from `sha256sum`) must be the same
  as the SHA-256 of the file above.
- `apksigner verify --print-certs interweave-{version}-arm64-v8a.apk`
  must show the SHA-256 of the signing certificate above. `apksigner`
  is part of the Android SDK Build Tools.

If either value is different, do not install the file. Delete it and
download it again from this page.

## Linux

Download [`{linux_archive}`]({linux_archive_url}).

It runs on Linux computers with a 64-bit x86 processor. On such a
computer, the command `uname -m` shows `x86_64`. There is no archive
for macOS, for Windows, or for Linux on an ARM processor.

Use this value to check that the archive is the one published here:

- SHA-256 of the archive: `{linux_sha256}`

The Linux archive has no signature, so this value is the only check.
In the folder that holds the archive, run `sha256sum {linux_archive}`.
It must show the same value. If the value is different, do not install
the archive. Delete it and download it again from this page.

### What the archive contains

- `human-desktop`: the InterWeave window, where you read and write
  messages.
- `transport-daemon`: the service that connects InterWeave to the
  network. It runs in the background, and the window works only while
  it runs.
- `transportctl`: a command-line tool for your identity, for the peers
  that you trust, and for recovery.
- systemd user units. One of them starts the service for you.
- A README with the installation steps, and the licence notices for
  the programs.

### Installing on Linux

Unpack the archive with `tar -xzf {linux_archive}`. Then follow the
steps in its README. You install InterWeave as your own user, in
`~/.local/bin`. You do not need root access.

In the README's steps, you choose a name for your profile. In the
commands below, replace `<profile>` with that name.

The first time, `transportctl --profile <profile> identity init`
creates your identity. It also shows the identity's PeerId: the name
by which other people's InterWeave knows you.

Then make a recovery backup with
`transportctl --profile <profile> identity backup`. The service must
be stopped while you make it. Keep the backup private, because anyone
who has it can use your identity. If you lose your identity and you
have no backup, nobody can recover it.

### Updating on Linux

To update, download a newer release from its page, and check its
SHA-256 as above. Then install its programs over the old ones, with
the steps in the newer archive's README. Your identity, your profile,
the peers that you trust, your other settings and your messages stay.
InterWeave does not check for updates, so it cannot tell you that a
new release exists.

### Going back to an older release on Linux

Do not install a release that is older than the newest release you
have used. Some older releases cannot use everything that a newer
release saved. Such a release does not start. It shows which file
stopped it, and it changes nothing. To use InterWeave again, install
the newest release you have used, or a newer one.

## Made with Slint

[![Made with Slint](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/slint-ui/slint/v1.18.1/logo/MadeWithSlint-logo-light.svg)](https://slint.dev)

The InterWeave interface is made with [Slint](https://slint.dev), on
Android and on Linux. InterWeave uses Slint under the Slint
Royalty-free License 2.0.

## Third-party notices for the Android app

The notices for the Linux programs are in the Linux archive.

The Android app contains Skia, a graphics library, and the libraries
that Skia includes. Each of them is under its own licence:

- Skia and skcms: BSD-3-Clause
- libjpeg-turbo: IJG License, BSD-3-Clause and zlib License
- FreeType: FreeType License
- libpng: PNG Reference Library License version 2
- zlib: zlib License
- Expat: MIT
- Wuffs: Apache-2.0
- Vulkan Memory Allocator: MIT
- cpu_features: BSD-2-Clause

The credit that FreeType asks for, and the licence texts, follow.

{freetype_credit}

{third_party_notice_texts}

The Android app also contains Rust libraries (called crates), in the
versions used to build this release. Each of them is under its own
licence. Their notices are in a separate file, attached to this
release. The file begins with a list: each line names a crate, its
version and its licence, and gives the labels of its licence texts
(T1, T2 and so on). The licence texts follow the list, under the
heading "Licence texts". The link to the file is below. Its SHA-256
is in the Android section, earlier on this page, after the two values
for the app.

{crate_notices}
